.TH MEMCG_SHRINKER 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
memcg_shrinker \- list kernel memory shrinkers by object count per memory cgroup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B memcg_shrinker
.RB [ \-n
.IR lines ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B memcg_shrinker
reads the kernel's shrinker debugfs interface and lists each pair of
shrinker and memory cgroup, largest object count first.
.PP
For every shrinker directory under
.IR /sys/kernel/debug/shrinker ,
the program reads the
.I count
file.
Each line of that file holds a memory cgroup ID followed by one object
count per NUMA node.
.B memcg_shrinker
takes the cgroup ID and the first count, which is the count for NUMA
node 0, from each line.
It sorts all entries by that count in descending order and prints one
line per entry.
Printing stops at the first entry whose count is zero, or after
.I lines
entries if
.B \-n
is given.
.PP
Each output line has three fields, separated by spaces:
.IP \(bu 3
the object count, left-aligned in a field 8 characters wide;
.IP \(bu 3
the name of the shrinker's debugfs directory, which is the shrinker name
followed by a numeric ID (for example
.BR sb\-xfs:vda1\-36 ),
left-aligned in a field 20 characters wide;
.IP \(bu 3
the memory cgroup.
.PP
No header line is printed.
.PP
To name the memory cgroup, the program walks the directory tree under
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
and matches the cgroup ID against the inode numbers of the directories
it finds.
A match is printed as the full path of the directory, for example
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice .
IDs 0 and 1 are printed as
.BR / .
An ID with no matching directory is printed as
.BI "unknown (" id ) \fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-lines " \fIlines\fP"
Print at most
.I lines
entries.
Without this option, or with a value of 0, every entry with a non-zero
count is printed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/debug/shrinker/*/count
Read to obtain the object counts of each shrinker.
.TP
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
Walked to map cgroup IDs to cgroup paths.
.SH NOTES
The shrinker debugfs interface exists only when the kernel is built with
.BR CONFIG_SHRINKER_DEBUG ,
which depends on
.BR CONFIG_DEBUG_FS ,
and debugfs must be mounted at
.IR /sys/kernel/debug .
The kernel creates the debugfs root with mode 0700 by default and the
.I count
files with mode 0440, so the program normally needs root privileges.
.PP
Errors from listing the directories of either tree are ignored.
If
.I /sys/kernel/debug/shrinker
does not exist or cannot be listed, the program prints nothing and
exits with status 0.
If
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
cannot be listed, cgroups other than the root are printed as
.BI "unknown (" id ) \fR.
.PP
Only the NUMA node 0 count is used.
On systems with more than one NUMA node, objects on other nodes are not
counted, and entries whose objects are all on other nodes are not
printed.
.PP
According to
.I \%Documentation/\:admin\-guide/\:mm/\:shrinker_debugfs.rst
in the Linux source, reading a
.I count
file makes the kernel call the shrinker's
.B count_objects()
callback for each memory cgroup and NUMA node.
The program never writes to the shrinkers'
.I scan
files, so it does not reclaim any objects.
.PP
.B memcg_shrinker
is a Python 3 script.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the ten largest entries:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# memcg_shrinker \-n 10
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cgroups (7)
.PP
In the Linux source:
.IP \(bu 3
.I \%Documentation/\:admin\-guide/\:mm/\:shrinker_debugfs.rst
.IP \(bu 3
